      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment of the OIML certificate.\n\nKey information:\n- Certificate number: R137/2012-B-NL1-18.05\n- Project number: 1901860\n- Page total: 5\n- Member state: The Netherlands\n- Issuing authority: NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1\n- Person responsible: C. Oosterman, Head Certification Board\n- Address: Hugo de Grootplein 1, 3314 EG Dordrecht, the Netherlands\n- Phone: +31 78 6332332\n- Email: certin@nmi.nl\n- Website: www.nmi.nl\n- Date issued: 24 September 2018\n- Scheme: B\n- Applicant and Manufacturer: Flow Meter Group B.V., Innovatieweg 32, 7007 CD Doetinchem, The Netherlands\n- Certified type: turbine gas meter, Types: FMT-L, FMT-S, FMT-Lx and FMT-Dc\n- Recommendation: R 137-1 (2012) \"Gas meters\" → canonical id \"R137-1\"? Hmm, the rule says canonical e.g. \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\". R137-1 is the edition... Actually \"R 137-1 (2012)\" — the recommendation is R137-1, edition 2012. But the schema says edition (int). 2012 is a year, not really an edition. Let me think. The example in the schema says: id (canonical, e.g. \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\"), edition (int), amendment, scheme, accuracy_classes[]. For R 137-1 (2012), I could do id: \"R137-1\", edition: 2012? Or id: \"R137\", edition... Hmm. R137-1 is the part 1 of R137. The canonical id would be \"R137-1\". Edition — the year 2012. I'll use id \"R137-1\" and edition 2012? The schema says edition is int, so 2012 works as int. Alternatively edition could be null. I think id: \"R137-1\", edition: 2012 is reasonable. Hmm, but the example given was \"R60\" — for R137-1 (2012), maybe id: \"R137-1\", edition: 2012.\n\nActually wait — let me reconsider. The system prompt example: `recommendation: id (canonical, e.g. \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\"), edition (int), ...`. For R137/2012 certificates, the recommendation is \"R 137-1 (2012)\". So id: \"R137-1\", edition: 2012. That seems fine.\n\n- Accuracy class: 1,0 → 1.0\n- Scheme: B\n\nTest reports:\n- NMi-14200104-01 dated 15 September 2014, 14 pages\n- NMi-14200702-01 dated 18 December 2014, 13 pages\n- NMi-14200712-01 dated 23 December 2014, 35 pages\n- VSL-3255499-01 dated 28 January 2015, 6 pages\n- NMi-1901860-02 dated 26 April 2018, 4 pages\n\nRole: these are reports that establish conformity — \"type_evaluation_report\" seems appropriate for these. The documentation folder no. T10466-7 is mentioned: \"The construction of the measuring instrument is recorded in the Documentation folder no. T10466-7.\" That could be a test_reports entry with role \"documentation_file\".\n\nTable 1 General characteristics (type_level):\n- Destined for the measurement of: Gas volume → measurement_quantity or destined_for: \"Gas volume\"\n- Mechanical class: M1\n- Electromagnetic class: Not applicable (the meter has no electronics)\n- Ambient temperature range: -40 °C / +70 °C → {min: -40, max: 70}, unit \"°C\"\n- Gas temperature range: -40 °C / +70 °C\n- Orientation: Horizontal / Vertical up / Vertical down (all orientations) → string\n- Flow direction: Uni-directional (indicated with arrow) → string\n\nTable 2: family of instruments — complex multi-dim table. Columns:\n- Type\n- DN body (mm)\n- DN cartr. (mm)\n- G-value\n- Qmax (m³/h)\n- Qt (m³/h)\n- Qmin under three measurement ratios (MR): MR 1:20 (0..101 bar(g)), MR 1:30 (4...101 bar(g)), MR 1:50 (8..101 bar(g)) — each in m³/h\n- Number of drums (Before and after comma)\n- Control element (m³/lh — this is probably m³/lh i.e. m³ per liter-hour? Actually \"0,02\" — control element value 0,02 m³? Hmm. \"m³/lh\" probably means m³ per litre... Actually control element 0,02 likely means the value of the control element in m³... The unit column says m³/lh which is weird OCR. Probably \"m³/h\"... hmm, control element could be in dm³? Whatever — keep as in the source.)\n- Blade angle (degree)\n- Blade qty\n- Bearing dimensions (Inside diameter x Outside diameter x Width in mm): front standard, rear standard, front optional, rear optional\n\nThe table has rowspan/colspan complexity. Each \"type/DN body/DN cartr\" group has 3 rows for 3 G-values.\n\nThis is a big matrix table. I should record it as matrix_tables[] preserving the rows, and also populate model_family.models and characteristics.model_level.\n\nModels: The model_id could be combination of Type + DN body + DN cartr + G-value. Let me enumerate the rows:\n\nGroup 1: Type FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc, DN body 50, DN cartr 80\n- Row 1: G=100, Qmax=160, Qt=32, Qmin(MR1:20)=8, Qmin(MR1:30)=5, Qmin(MR1:50)=3, drums 7/1, control 0.02, blade angle 55, blade qty 12, bearings std front 3x6x3, std rear 2x5x2, opt front 3x8x4, opt rear 3x6x3\n- Row 2: G=160, Qmax=250, Qt=50, Qmin=12.5, 8, 5, angle 45, qty 14\n- Row 3: G=250, Qmax=400, Qt=80, Qmin=20, 12.5, 8, angle 35, qty 16\n\nGroup 2: Type FMT-L, FMT-S, FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc, DN body 80, DN cartr 80\n- Same as group 1 rows: G=100/160/250, same values, same bearings.\n\nGroup 3: FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc, DN body 80, DN cartr 100\n- Row 1: G=160, Qmax=250, Qt=50, Qmin=12.5/8/5, drums 7/1, ctrl 0.02, angle 55, qty 12, bearings: std front 3x8x4, std rear 3x6x3, opt front 5x10x3, opt rear 3x6x3\n- Row 2: G=250, Qmax=400, Qt=80, Qmin=20/12.5/8, angle 45, qty 14\n- Row 3: G=400, Qmax=650, Qt=130, Qmin=32.5/20/12.5, angle 35, qty 16\n\nGroup 4: FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc, DN body 100, DN cartr 80\n- Row 1: G=100, Qmax=160, Qt=32, Qmin=8/5/3, angle 55/12, bearings std 3x6x3, 2x5x2, opt 3x8x4, 3x6x3\n- Row 2: G=160, Qmax=250, Qt=50, 12.5/8/5, 45/14\n- Row 3: G=250, Qmax=400, Qt=80, 20/12.5/8, 35/16\n\nGroup 5: FMT-L, FMT-S, FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc, DN body 100, DN cartr 100\n- Row 1: G=160, Qmax=250, Qt=50, 12.5/8/5, 55/12, bearings 3x8x4, 3x6x3, 5x10x3, 3x6x3\n- Row 2: G=250, Qmax=400, Qt=80, 20/12.5/8, 45/14\n- Row 3: G=400, Qmax=650, Qt=130, 32.5/20/12.5, 35/16\n\nGroup 6: FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc, DN body 100, DN cartr 150\n- Row 1: G=400, Qmax=650, Qt=130, Qmin=32.5/20/12.5, drums 7/1, ctrl 0.02, angle 55, qty 12, bearings 5x11x5, 5x11x5, 5x16x5, 5x11x5\n- Row 2: G=650, Qmax=1000, Qt=200, Qmin=50/32/20, drums 7/1, ctrl 0.02, angle 45, qty 14\n- Row 3: G=1000, Qmax=1600, Qt=320, Qmin=80/50/32, drums 8/0, ctrl 0.2, angle 35, qty 16\n\nGroup 7: FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc, DN body 150, DN cartr 100\n- Row 1: G=160, Qmax=250, Qt=50, 12.5/8/5, 7/1, 0.02, 55/12, bearings 3x8x4, 3x6x3, 5x10x3, 3x6x3\n- Row 2: G=250, 400, 80, 20/12.5/8, 45/14\n- Row 3: G=400, 650, 130, 32.5/20/12.5, 35/16\n\nGroup 8: FMT-L, FMT-S, FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc, DN body 150, DN cartr 150\n- Row 1: G=400, 650, 130, 32.5/20/12.5, 7/1, 0.02, 55/12, bearings 5x11x5, 5x11x5, 5x16x5, 5x11x5\n- Row 2: G=650, 1000, 200, 50/32/20, 7/1, 0.02, 45/14\n- Row 3: G=1000, 1600, 320, 80/50/32, 8/0, 0.2, 35/16\n\nGroup 9: FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc, DN body 150, DN cartr 200\n- Row 1: G=650, 1000, 200, 50/32/20, 7/1, 0.02, 55/12, bearings 5x16x5, 5x16x5, 8x22x7, 5x16x5\n- Row 2: G=1000, 1600, 320, 80/50/32, 8/0, 0.2, 45/14\n- Row 3: G=1600, 2500, 500, 125/80/50, 8/0, 0.2, 35/16\n\nGroup 10: FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc, DN body 200, DN cartr 150\n- Row 1: G=400, 650, 130, 32.5/20/12.5, 7/1, 0.02, 55/12, bearings 5x11x5, 5x11x5, 5x16x5, 5x11x5\n- Row 2: G=650, 1000, 200, 50/32/20, 8/0, 0.2, 45/14\n- Row 3: G=1000, 1600, 320, 80/50/32, 8/0, 0.2, 35/16\n\nGroup 11: FMT-L, FMT-S, FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc, DN body 200, DN cartr 200\n- Row 1: G=650, 1000, 200, 50/32/20, 7/1, 0.02, 55/12, bearings 5x16x5, 5x16x5, 8x22x7, 5x16x5\n- Row 2: G=1000, 1600, 320, 80/50/32, 7/1, 0.02, 45/14\n- Row 3: G=1600, 2500, 500, 125/80/50, 8/0, 0.2, 35/16\n\nGroup 12: FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc, DN body 250, DN cartr 200\n- Row 1: G=650, 1000, 200, 50/32/20, 7/1, 0.02, 55/12, bearings 5x16x5, 5x16x5, 8x22x7, 5x16x5\n- Row 2: G=1000, 1600, 320, 80/50/32, 8/0, 0.2, 45/14\n- Row 3: G=1600, 2500, 500, 125/80/50, 8/0, 0.2, 35/16\n\nSo 12 groups × 3 rows = 36 rows.\n\nNote group 10 (DN 200/150) row 2 has 8/0 and 0.2, unlike group 8 (150/150) row 2 which has 7/1 0.02. Let me double check from the markdown:\n\nGroup 10 (FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc, 200, 150):\n```\nRow1: 400, 650, 130, 32,5, 20, 12,5 — 7/1, 0,02, 55, 12 — bearings 5x11x5, 5x11x5, 5x16x5, 5x11x5\nRow2: 650, 1000, 200, 50, 32, 20 — 8 / 0, 0,2, 45, 14\nRow3: 1000, 1600, 320, 80, 50, 32 — 8 / 0, 0,2, 35, 16\n```\nYes.\n\nGroup 11 (FMT-L, FMT-S, FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc, 200, 200):\n```\nRow1: 650, 1000, 200, 50, 32, 20 — 7/1, 0,02, 55, 12 — bearings 5x16x5, 5x16x5, 8x22x7, 5x16x5\nRow2: 1000, 1600, 320, 80, 50, 32 — 7 / 1, 0,02, 45, 14\nRow3: 1600, 2500, 500, 125, 80, 50 — 8 / 0, 0,2, 35, 16\n```\nYes.\n\nGroup 12 (FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc, 250, 200):\n```\nRow1: 650, 1000, 200, 50, 32, 20 — 7/1, 0,02, 55, 12\nRow2: 1000, 1600, 320, 80, 50, 32 — 8 / 0, 0,2, 45, 14\nRow3: 1600, 2500, 500, 125, 80, 50 — 8 / 0, 0,2, 35, 16\n```\n\nOK.\n\nNow, the model_family: models would be 36 variants. That's a lot but let's do it. Model id: something like \"FMT-Lx DN50/80 G100\" — hmm, but type is \"FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\". Maybe model_id like \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN50 DN80 G100\". Alternatively use a sequential ID. Let me use a composite: `FMT-Lx|FMT-Dc_DN50/80_G100`. Hmm, cleaner: \"DN50/80 G100 (FMT-Lx, FMT-Dc)\". \n\nActually let me think about what's most useful. The model_id should identify the variant. A natural composite: `{types}_DN{body}_{cartridge}_G{value}`. E.g. \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN50_80 G100\". I'll go with format: `\"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN50/80 G100\"` — but the slash in DN50/80 is confusing with the type alternation. Maybe: `\"FMT-Lx,FMT-Dc DN50 DN80 G100\"`. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively simpler: `\"model-01\"` ... that loses information. The schema example used `\"Emax=25t\"` style — attributes encoded. I'll use: `\"FMT-Lx|FMT-Dc DN50/80 G100\"`. Actually let me just use a hyphen-based approach: model_id like `\"FMT-Lx-DN50-80-G100\"`. But \"FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\" is a set of alternatives, not a single type. I could encode as \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc\". \n\nI'll use model_id strings like:\n- \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN50 DN80 G100\"\n- \"FMT-L/FMT-S/FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN80 DN80 G100\"\n\nThat's verbose but unambiguous.\n\nHmm, 36 models with long IDs. That's fine.\n\nThen model_level characteristics: For each attribute that varies across models: g_value, qmax, qt, qmin, drums, control_element, blade_angle, blade_qty, bearing dims, dn_body, dn_cartridge, type... That would be enormous — 10 attributes × 36 values each. That's a lot of JSON but it's the correct per the schema. Hmm, the schema example for R137 says extract matrix_tables AND populate model_family.models and characteristics.model_level with qmax/qt/qmin.\n\nGiven the size, I should be pragmatic. The matrix_tables preserves everything. For model_level, I could include the key flow attributes: qmax, qt, qmin. But per rule 4, varying attributes should go in model_level. However, emitting all 10+ attributes × 36 models would be huge. The instructions say to populate model_level — I think I should do it for the main characteristics. Let me think about output size. 36 models × ~10 attributes: each attribute entry has 36 value objects. 10 attributes → 360 small objects. That's maybe 15-20KB of JSON. Manageable.\n\nBut also model_family.models with 36 entries each having attributes{} — that would duplicate. The schema says models[]: {model_id, label, attributes{}}. I could put attributes in each model entry instead of model_level, or both. The R60 example only shows {\"model_id\": \"Emax=25t\"} without attributes. The R137 example also shows just model_id. So I'll keep models[] minimal (just model_id), and put the varying attributes in model_level, and the full table in matrix_tables.\n\nActually, to avoid gigantic duplication, maybe I put the full data in matrix_tables (name, columns, column_units, rows) and model_level for the principal flow characteristics (qmax, qt, qmin) as shown in the R137 example. The example explicitly shows only qmax, qt, qmin in model_level while the matrix table has all columns. I'll follow that pattern but maybe add a few more (g_value, drums, control_element, blade_angle, blade_qty). Hmm.\n\nLet me decide: model_level attributes to emit:\n- g_value (unit null)\n- qmax (m³/h)\n- qt (m³/h)\n- qmin_min? Actually qmin varies by measurement ratio (config axis!). The table has three Qmin columns depending on MR (measurement ratio): MR 1:20, MR 1:30, MR 1:50. That's a config axis. So qmin could be config_level: attribute qmin, axis measurement_ratio, values: [{condition: \"1:20\", model: ..., value...}]. Hmm, config_level values have {condition, value}. But here it's both model AND condition. The schema's config_level doesn't have model in values. Hmm.\n\nOption: keep qmin in the matrix table only (since it's 2-D: model × measurement-ratio), and optionally emit three model_level attributes: qmin_at_mr_1_20, qmin_at_mr_1_30, qmin_at_mr_1_50. That's clean. The MR conditions: MR 1:20 (0..101 bar(g)), MR 1:30 (4...101 bar(g)), MR 1:50 (8..101 bar(g)). Wait — MR 1:20 says \"0..101 bar(g)\" and MR 1:30 says \"4...101 bar(g)\", MR 1:50 \"8..101 bar(g)\". Interesting — these are pressure ranges. Hmm wait, actually those pressure ranges look odd for a MR (measuring ratio). R137 defines Qmin depends on the measurement ratio range. Actually for turbine meters, MR = Qmax/Qmin. So MR 1:20 gives wider range at lower pressure... Hmm, the bar(g) ranges probably relate to pressure at which that MR applies. Actually re-reading: \"Qmin\" with three sub-columns \"MR 1:20 0..101 bar(g)\", \"MR 1:30 4...101 bar(g)\", \"MR 1:50 8..101 bar(g)\". So for a given pressure range, the achievable measurement ratio differs. E.g., across 0–101 bar you can guarantee MR 1:20; across 4–101 bar you get 1:30; across 8–101 bar you get 1:50. So Qmin values differ.\n\nI'll emit model_level attributes: qmin_mr_1_20, qmin_mr_1_30, qmin_mr_1_50 with unit m³/h. And maybe also record in matrix table.\n\n- number_of_drums: \"7 / 1\" — string like \"7/1\". Attribute number_of_drums_before_and_after_comma? Canonical: `number_of_drums_before_and_after_decimal_point`? The header: \"Number of drums Before and after comma\" → number_of_drums. Value \"7/1\". Keep as string \"7/1\" since it's two numbers (before/after). Or split into drums_before_comma and drums_after_comma. Splitting is nicer: `drums_before_decimal_point` and `drums_after_decimal_point`. Hmm, values 7/1 and 8/0. I could do two model_level attributes. That's cleaner as numbers. But it's also fine to keep \"7/1\" as string. Given the strict-value rules (numbers not strings), splitting is better. I'll do `number_of_drums_before_comma` and `number_of_drums_after_comma`. Hmm, but \"comma\" is European terminology for decimal point. I'll use `drums_before_decimal_mark`... Let me keep it simple: `number_of_drums_before_comma` / `number_of_drums_after_comma` (matching source wording). Actually the control element relates: with 7/1 drums and control element 0.02 → 8/0 with 0.2. Whatever.\n\n- control_element: values 0.02 or 0.2, unit \"m³/lh\"? The unit column under Control element says \"m³/lh\" — that's likely OCR of \"m³/h\"?? No wait, control element 0,02 — hmm. Actually for drum-type gas meters, the \"control element\" might be in m³ per revolution... \"m³/lh\" might be OCR garbage for \"m³/U\" (per revolution) or \"dm³\". I'll keep unit_symbol as \"m³/lh\" as-is from source? That seems wrong. Hmm. Rule: don't fabricate; use raw symbol when uncertain. The unit column shows \"m³/lh\". I'll record unit_symbol \"m³/lh\". Hmm, but that propagates an OCR error. Alternatively null. I think keeping the raw source symbol is the safer, rule-compliant choice (like %vol example). I'll use \"m³/lh\".\n\nHmm wait, maybe it's \"m³/h\"? No — control element of 0.02 m³/h doesn't make sense. For a drum meter, control element = volume per drum revolution, e.g. 0.02 m³. Hmm. The \"/\" might be a division: m³ per litre-hour? Unclear. Keep raw.\n\n- blade_angle (unit \"degree\")\n- blade_qty (count, unit null)\n- bearing dimensions: these are strings like \"3x6x3\" (inside dia × outside dia × width in mm). Four columns: standard front, standard rear, optional front, optional rear. I could emit model_level attributes: bearing_standard_front, bearing_standard_rear, bearing_optional_front, bearing_optional_rear with values as strings \"3x6x3\" and maybe note unit mm. Value is a compound dimension string — keep as string. That's 4 more model_level entries.\n\n- dn_body (mm), dn_cartridge (mm): constant per group, vary across models. Emit model_level.\n\nThat's a lot. Total model_level entries: type? dn_body, dn_cartridge, g_value, qmax, qt, qmin_mr_1_20, qmin_mr_1_30, qmin_mr_1_50, number_of_drums_before_comma, number_of_drums_after_comma, control_element, blade_angle, blade_qty, bearing_standard_front, bearing_standard_rear, bearing_optional_front, bearing_optional_rear = 17 entries × 36 values = 612 objects. Big but doable? That might make the response extremely long. Each value object ~ {\"model\": \"...\", \"value\": 160} ~ 40 chars → 612 × ~50 chars ≈ 30KB plus model ids which are long (~30 chars each). Could reach 40-50KB. That's quite long but within output limits probably. Hmm.\n\nAlternative: rely on matrix_tables rows for full data, and model_level for the main ones (dn_body, dn_cartridge, g_value, qmax, qt, qmin×3). The guidance's R137 example only put qmax/qt/qmin in model_level even though the table had more columns. I'll follow that pattern but add g_value since G-value is the defining size attribute, and dn values. Let me include: g_value, qmax, qt, qmin_mr_1_20, qmin_mr_1_30, qmin_mr_1_50. That's 6 × 36 = 216 objects. Plus matrix_tables with 36 rows carrying everything.\n\nHmm, but then number_of_drums/control element/blade info only in matrix table. I think that's acceptable given the guidance example. Actually, let me reconsider — should I include drums/control/blade/bearings in model_level too for completeness? The schema says \"attribute × model matrix\" for varying attributes. The matrix_tables already preserve them (\"preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\"). The blade/drums vary per model too, so they fit model_level... but the example chose only flow characteristics. I'll compromise: include drums (as strings \"7/1\"? no...), hmm.\n\nDecision: model_level will include: qmax, qt, qmin (as three MR-specific attributes), g_value, dn_body, dn_cartridge. The rest (drums, control element, blade, bearings) stay in matrix_tables only. This balances fidelity and size. Actually, dn_body and dn_cartridge are already encoded in the model_id... but having them as attributes is fine and structured. Hmm, maybe skip dn since it's in model_id and matrix. But encoding in model_id alone isn't machine-friendly. I'll include them — it's only 72 more small objects... Actually, each model_id will contain DN info, so dn_body entries are somewhat redundant but harmless. Let me include them to be safe? I'll include g_value, qmax, qt, qmin×3 — and keep dn in model_id + matrix table. Hmm.\n\nActually, you know, let me reconsider the model_id format to make DN parseable: \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN50/80 G100\" — DN50 body, 80 cartridge. And \"FMT-L/FMT-S/FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN80/80 G100\". OK.\n\nLet me also decide whether models should have label. Optional; omit or set label like \"DN50 body / DN80 cartridge / G100\". I'll include label for readability. Hmm, that adds 36 more strings. Fine.\n\nNow matrix_tables: name \"General characteristics of the family of instruments\" (Table 2). Columns: type, dn_body_mm, dn_cartridge_mm, g_value, qmax_m3_h, qt_m3_h, qmin_mr_1_20_m3_h, qmin_mr_1_30_m3_h, qmin_mr_1_50_m3_h, number_of_drums_before_and_after_comma, control_element_m3_lh, blade_angle_degree, blade_qty, bearing_standard_front_mm, bearing_standard_rear_mm, bearing_optional_front_mm, bearing_optional_rear_mm.\n\nColumn units: type: null; dn_body: \"mm\"; dn_cartridge: \"mm\"; g_value: null; qmax: \"m³/h\"; qt: \"m³/h\"; qmin×3: \"m³/h\"; drums: null; control_element: \"m³/lh\"; blade_angle: \"degree\"; blade_qty: null; bearings: \"mm\".\n\nRows: 36 objects. Each row: type (list? type is \"FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\"). I'll put type as string \"FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\" or array? Object per row with columns as keys — type value as string \"FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\". Hmm, maybe an array [\"FMT-Lx\",\"FMT-Dc\"] is more structured. The rows are \"object per row\" — values can be arrays. I'll use arrays for the type column: [\"FMT-Lx\", \"FMT-Dc\"] or [\"FMT-L\", \"FMT-S\", \"FMT-Lx\", \"FMT-Dc\"]. That's cleaner. Hmm, but might complicate. I'll use arrays; it's JSON-valid.\n\nNumbers: convert commas: 12,5 → 12.5; 32,5 → 32.5; 0,02 → 0.02; 0,2 → 0.2.\n\nLet me now write all 36 rows carefully.\n\nGroup 1: types [FMT-Lx, FMT-Dc], dn_body 50, dn_cartr 80:\n- G100: qmax 160, qt 32, qmin 8/5/3, drums 7/1, ctrl 0.02, angle 55, qty 12, bearings std 3x6x3 / 2x5x2, opt 3x8x4 / 3x6x3\n- G160: qmax 250, qt 50, qmin 12.5/8/5, angle 45, qty 14\n- G250: qmax 400, qt 80, qmin 20/12.5/8, angle 35, qty 16\n\nGroup 2: types [FMT-L, FMT-S, FMT-Lx, FMT-Dc], dn_body 80, dn_cartr 80: same as group 1.\n- G100: 160/32/8/5/3, 7/1, 0.02, 55, 12, 3x6x3/2x5x2/3x8x4/3x6x3\n- G160: 250/50/12.5/8/5, 45, 14\n- G250: 400/80/20/12.5/8, 35, 16\n\nGroup 3: [FMT-Lx, FMT-Dc], dn 80/100:\n- G160: 250/50/12.5/8/5, 7/1, 0.02, 55, 12, 3x8x4/3x6x3/5x10x3/3x6x3\n- G250: 400/80/20/12.5/8, 45, 14\n- G400: 650/130/32.5/20/12.5, 35, 16\n\nGroup 4: [FMT-Lx, FMT-Dc], dn 100/80:\n- G100: 160/32/8/5/3, 7/1, 0.02, 55, 12, 3x6x3/2x5x2/3x8x4/3x6x3\n- G160: 250/50/12.5/8/5, 45, 14\n- G250: 400/80/20/12.5/8, 35, 16\n\nGroup 5: [FMT-L, FMT-S, FMT-Lx, FMT-Dc], dn 100/100:\n- G160: 250/50/12.5/8/5, 7/1, 0.02, 55, 12, 3x8x4/3x6x3/5x10x3/3x6x3\n- G250: 400/80/20/12.5/8, 45, 14\n- G400: 650/130/32.5/20/12.5, 35, 16\n\nGroup 6: [FMT-Lx, FMT-Dc], dn 100/150:\n- G400: 650/130/32.5/20/12.5, 7/1, 0.02, 55, 12, 5x11x5/5x11x5/5x16x5/5x11x5\n- G650: 1000/200/50/32/20, 7/1, 0.02, 45, 14\n- G1000: 1600/320/80/50/32, 8/0, 0.2, 35, 16\n\nGroup 7: [FMT-Lx, FMT-Dc], dn 150/100:\n- G160: 250/50/12.5/8/5, 7/1, 0.02, 55, 12, 3x8x4/3x6x3/5x10x3/3x6x3\n- G250: 400/80/20/12.5/8, 45, 14\n- G400: 650/130/32.5/20/12.5, 35, 16\n\nGroup 8: [FMT-L, FMT-S, FMT-Lx, FMT-Dc], dn 150/150:\n- G400: 650/130/32.5/20/12.5, 7/1, 0.02, 55, 12, 5x11x5/5x11x5/5x16x5/5x11x5\n- G650: 1000/200/50/32/20, 7/1, 0.02, 45, 14\n- G1000: 1600/320/80/50/32, 8/0, 0.2, 35, 16\n\nGroup 9: [FMT-Lx, FMT-Dc], dn 150/200:\n- G650: 1000/200/50/32/20, 7/1, 0.02, 55, 12, 5x16x5/5x16x5/8x22x7/5x16x5\n- G1000: 1600/320/80/50/32, 8/0, 0.2, 45, 14\n- G1600: 2500/500/125/80/50, 8/0, 0.2, 35, 16\n\nGroup 10: [FMT-Lx, FMT-Dc], dn 200/150:\n- G400: 650/130/32.5/20/12.5, 7/1, 0.02, 55, 12, 5x11x5/5x11x5/5x16x5/5x11x5\n- G650: 1000/200/50/32/20, 8/0, 0.2, 45, 14\n- G1000: 1600/320/80/50/32, 8/0, 0.2, 35, 16\n\nGroup 11: [FMT-L, FMT-S, FMT-Lx, FMT-Dc], dn 200/200:\n- G650: 1000/200/50/32/20, 7/1, 0.02, 55, 12, 5x16x5/5x16x5/8x22x7/5x16x5\n- G1000: 1600/320/80/50/32, 7/1, 0.02, 45, 14\n- G1600: 2500/500/125/80/50, 8/0, 0.2, 35, 16\n\nGroup 12: [FMT-Lx, FMT-Dc], dn 250/200:\n- G650: 1000/200/50/32/20, 7/1, 0.02, 55, 12, 5x16x5/5x16x5/8x22x7/5x16x5\n- G1000: 1600/320/80/50/32, 8/0, 0.2, 45, 14\n- G1600: 2500/500/125/80/50, 8/0, 0.2, 35, 16\n\nGood, 36 rows.\n\nModel IDs: I'll use format like \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN50/80 G100\". For the four-type rows: \"FMT-L/FMT-S/FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN80/80 G100\". \n\nLet me generate the 36 model ids:\n1. FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN50/80 G100\n2. FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN50/80 G160\n3. FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN50/80 G250\n4. FMT-L/FMT-S/FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN80/80 G100\n5. FMT-L/FMT-S/FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN80/80 G160\n6. FMT-L/FMT-S/FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN80/80 G250\n7. FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN80/100 G160\n8. FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN80/100 G250\n9. FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN80/100 G400\n10. FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN100/80 G100\n11. FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN100/80 G160\n12. FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN100/80 G250\n13. FMT-L/FMT-S/FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN100/100 G160\n14. FMT-L/FMT-S/FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN100/100 G250\n15. FMT-L/FMT-S/FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN100/100 G400\n16. FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN100/150 G400\n17. FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN100/150 G650\n18. FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN100/150 G1000\n19. FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN150/100 G160\n20. FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN150/100 G250\n21. FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN150/100 G400\n22. FMT-L/FMT-S/FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN150/150 G400\n23. FMT-L/FMT-S/FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN150/150 G650\n24. FMT-L/FMT-S/FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN150/150 G1000\n25. FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN150/200 G650\n26. FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN150/200 G1000\n27. FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN150/200 G1600\n28. FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN200/150 G400\n29. FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN200/150 G650\n30. FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN200/150 G1000\n31. FMT-L/FMT-S/FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN200/200 G650\n32. FMT-L/FMT-S/FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN200/200 G1000\n33. FMT-L/FMT-S/FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN200/200 G1600\n34. FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN250/200 G650\n35. FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN250/200 G1000\n36. FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN250/200 G1600\n\nNow certificate-level:\n- number: \"R137/2012-B-NL1-18.05\"\n- scheme: \"B\"\n- project_number: \"1901860\"\n- page_total: 5\n- member_state: \"The Netherlands\"\n- date_issued: \"2018-09-24\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\" (from \"OIML Issuing Authority NL1\")\n\nissuing_authority:\n- name: \"NMi Certin B.V.\"\n- address_lines: [\"Hugo de Grootplein 1\", \"3314 EG Dordrecht\", \"the Netherlands\"]\n- person_responsible: \"C. Oosterman\"\n- person_title: \"Head Certification Board\"\n- phone: \"+31 78 6332332\"\n- email: \"certin@nmi.nl\"\n- website: \"www.nmi.nl\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"\n\napplicants: [{name: \"Flow Meter Group B.V.\", address_lines: [\"Innovatieweg 32\", \"7007 CD Doetinchem\", \"The Netherlands\"]}]\n\nmanufacturers: same (labeled \"Applicant and Manufacturer\").\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Gas meter\" (per the allowed examples; \"turbine gas meter\")\n- type_designations: [\"FMT-L\", \"FMT-S\", \"FMT-Lx\", \"FMT-Dc\"]\n- module_designation: null or omit (no electronics; the meter is purely mechanical). \"Electromagnetic class: Not applicable (the meter has no electronics)\" — no modules. I'll omit or null. I'll set null? Rule 8: omit if not present. The field module_designation isn't present. Omit.\n- description: \"Turbine gas meter\" — the identification says \"A turbine gas meter\". I'll set description: \"Turbine gas meter\".\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: null\n- models: 36 entries. Maybe include label with DN info. I'll include label like \"DN50 body, DN80 cartridge, G100\". Adds bulk... maybe skip labels to reduce size. The schema says label optional-ish (models[]: model_id, label, attributes{}). I'll include labels since they help. Hmm, 36 labels adds ~2KB. OK include.\n\nActually, to reduce redundancy maybe just model_id. The examples show only model_id. I'll include just model_id to keep it cleaner.\n\ncharacteristics:\n- type_level:\n  - destined_for / measurement_quantity: \"Gas volume\" → attribute `destined_for_measurement_of`: {value: \"Gas volume\", ...}. Structured form requires value/unit objects. Strings allowed.\n  - mechanical_class: {value: \"M1\"}\n  - electromagnetic_class: {value: \"Not applicable (the meter has no electronics)\"}\n  - ambient_temperature_range: {value: {min: -40, max: 70}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n  - gas_temperature_range: {value: {min: -40, max: 70}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n  - orientation: {value: \"Horizontal / Vertical up / Vertical down (all orientations)\"}\n  - flow_direction: {value: \"Uni-directional (indicated with arrow)\"}\n\n- model_level: as decided: qmax, qt, qmin_mr_1_20, qmin_mr_1_30, qmin_mr_1_50, g_value. Should I add dn_body, dn_cartridge? I'll add them — they're structural attributes that vary. Actually the model_id encodes them; adding gives 72 more value objects. Total model_level: 8 attrs × 36 = 288 objects. Fine, I'll include dn_body and dn_cartridge too? Hmm... Let me think about response length. Each value object: `{\"model\": \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN50/80 G100\", \"value\": 160}` ≈ 55 chars. 288 × 55 ≈ 16KB just for model_level. Plus matrix table 36 rows × ~350 chars ≈ 13KB. Plus header stuff. Total maybe 35-45KB. Should be OK.\n\nHmm, but do I really need dn_body/dn_cartridge in model_level given model_id contains them? I'll skip them to trim; model_id + matrix table carry it. Actually... for machine consumption, model_level attribute entries are the structured way. The example in the R137 case included \"size\" in models (model_id = \"G1.6\") and only flow attrs in model_level. Following that pattern, size (analog of DN) was in model_id only. So I'll skip dn in model_level. \n\nInclude in model_level: g_value, qmax, qt, qmin_mr_1_20, qmin_mr_1_30, qmin_mr_1_50. 6 attrs.\n\nAlso maybe number_of_drums / control_element etc.? I'll leave them in matrix_tables only per the example pattern.\n\nHmm, one more consideration: should qmin be a single model_level attribute with config_level for MR? The schema config_level: {attribute, axis, values: [{condition, value}]}. The condition here would need to combine model + pressure range. It's cleaner as three attributes with MR in the name: qmin_mr_1_20 etc. The MR conditions also have pressure ranges: MR 1:20 applies 0..101 bar(g), MR 1:30 4..101 bar(g), MR 1:50 8..101 bar(g). I can note this in the matrix table description or in attribute naming. I'll add a matrix_tables description mentioning that Qmin is given per measurement-ratio option with the applicable pressure range, or add config_level? Let me add config_level entries for the pressure range conditions? That conflates axes. Simpler: name attributes `qmin_mr_1_20` and put the pressure-range mapping in the matrix table description and maybe in the attribute... no. I'll put a \"description\" on the matrix table: \"Qmin is specified per measurement ratio (MR) option: MR 1:20 over 0–101 bar(g), MR 1:30 over 4–101 bar(g), MR 1:50 over 8–101 bar(g). Bearing dimensions are inside diameter × outside diameter × width.\"\n\nAlternatively, I can add config_level:\n{attribute: \"qmin\", axis: \"measurement_ratio_pressure_range\", values: [{condition: \"MR 1:20, 0–101 bar(g)\", ...}]} — but values lack model linkage, so it doesn't work. Skip config_level; use the three attribute names.\n\ntest_reports:\n- {id: \"NMi-14200104-01\", date: \"2014-09-15\", pages: 14, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n- {id: \"NMi-14200702-01\", date: \"2014-12-18\", pages: 13, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n- {id: \"NMi-14200712-01\", date: \"2014-12-23\", pages: 35, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n- {id: \"VSL-3255499-01\", date: \"2015-01-28\", pages: 6, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n- {id: \"NMi-1901860-02\", date: \"2018-04-26\", pages: 4, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n- {id: \"T10466-7\", role: \"documentation_file\"} — \"Documentation folder no. T10466-7\". Include with role documentation_file, no date/pages.\n\nrecommendation:\n- id: \"R137-1\"? or \"R137\"? The doc says R 137-1 (2012) \"Gas meters\". Canonical: \"R137-1\". edition: 2012? edition as int — 2012 is the year. In OIML, R 137-1 has edition 2012. I'll put edition: 2012. Hmm, \"edition (int)\" — fine.\n- scheme: \"B\"\n- accuracy_classes: [1.0]\n\nAccuracy class \"1,0\" → 1.0.\n\nrevision_history: not present. Footnotes: none present in fragment.\n\nMatrix table rows: let me write them.\n\nRow keys: type, dn_body, dn_cartridge, g_value, qmax, qt, qmin_mr_1_20, qmin_mr_1_30, qmin_mr_1_50, drums, control_element, blade_angle, blade_qty, bearing_standard_front, bearing_standard_rear, bearing_optional_front, bearing_optional_rear.\n\nHmm — drums: \"7 / 1\" → I'll write as \"7/1\" string, or split? In matrix rows, keep \"7/1\". Or split into drums_before and drums_after for numeric cleanliness. In the matrix row I'll keep the combined string \"7/1\"? The rule says numbers not strings, but \"7/1\" is two values; a string is acceptable within a matrix row. Alternatively split. I'll split into `drums_before_comma` and `drums_after_comma` with numeric values — cleaner and machine-friendly. Actually the header says \"Number of drums Before and after comma\". So two columns: number_of_drums_before_comma, number_of_drums_after_comma. Values: 7/1, 8/0 etc.\n\nColumns list for matrix_tables[\"columns\"]: [\"type\", \"dn_body\", \"dn_cartridge\", \"g_value\", \"qmax\", \"qt\", \"qmin_mr_1_20\", \"qmin_mr_1_30\", \"qmin_mr_1_50\", \"number_of_drums_before_comma\", \"number_of_drums_after_comma\", \"control_element\", \"blade_angle\", \"blade_qty\", \"bearing_standard_front\", \"bearing_standard_rear\", \"bearing_optional_front\", \"bearing_optional_rear\"]\n\ncolumn_units: [null, \"mm\", \"mm\", null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null, null, \"m³/lh\", \"degree\", null, \"mm\", \"mm\", \"mm\", \"mm\"]\n\nControl element unit — source shows \"m³/lh\". Hmm, \"m³/lh\" ... Actually! It might be \"m³/h\" with a stray l, i.e. OCR artifact. But 0.02 m³/h as a \"control element\" — control element likely is a volume (per drum revolution). Whatever, keep raw \"m³/lh\". Hmm, wait — maybe it's actually \"m³/rev\"? No. Keep raw.\n\nType column values: arrays. E.g. [\"FMT-Lx\", \"FMT-Dc\"].\n\nNow writing all 36 rows (compact):\n\nGroup 1 (FMT-Lx/Dc, 50, 80):\nr1: {\"type\": [\"FMT-Lx\",\"FMT-Dc\"], \"dn_body\": 50, \"dn_cartridge\": 80, \"g_value\": 100, \"qmax\": 160, \"qt\": 32, \"qmin_mr_1_20\": 8, \"qmin_mr_1_30\": 5, \"qmin_mr_1_50\": 3, \"number_of_drums_before_comma\": 7, \"number_of_drums_after_comma\": 1, \"control_element\": 0.02, \"blade_angle\": 55, \"blade_qty\": 12, \"bearing_standard_front\": \"3x6x3\", \"bearing_standard_rear\": \"2x5x2\", \"bearing_optional_front\": \"3x8x4\", \"bearing_optional_rear\": \"3x6x3\"}\nr2: g 160, qmax 250, qt 50, qmin 12.5/8/5, angle 45, qty 14 (same drums/bearings)\nr3: g 250, qmax 400, qt 80, qmin 20/12.5/8, angle 35, qty 16\n\nGroup 2 (all four types, 80, 80): rows same as group 1.\nGroup 3 (Lx/Dc, 80, 100): r1 g160: qmax 250, qt 50, 12.5/8/5, 7/1, 0.02, 55, 12, 3x8x4/3x6x3/5x10x3/3x6x3; r2 g250: 400/80/20/12.5/8, 45/14; r3 g400: 650/130/32.5/20/12.5, 35/16.\nGroup 4 (Lx/Dc, 100, 80): same as group1 rows (g100/160/250, bearings 3x6x3/2x5x2/3x8x4/3x6x3).\nGroup 5 (all four, 100, 100): same as group 3 (g160/250/400, bearings 3x8x4/3x6x3/5x10x3/3x6x3).\nGroup 6 (Lx/Dc, 100, 150): g400: 650/130/32.5/20/12.5, 7/1, 0.02, 55, 12, 5x11x5/5x11x5/5x16x5/5x11x5; g650: 1000/200/50/32/20, 7/1, 0.02, 45, 14; g1000: 1600/320/80/50/32, 8/0, 0.2, 35, 16.\nGroup 7 (Lx/Dc, 150, 100): same as group 3 values.\nGroup 8 (all four, 150, 150): same as group 6.\nGroup 9 (Lx/Dc, 150, 200): g650: 1000/200/50/32/20, 7/1, 0.02, 55, 12, 5x16x5/5x16x5/8x22x7/5x16x5; g1000: 1600/320/80/50/32, 8/0, 0.2, 45, 14; g1600: 2500/500/125/80/50, 8/0, 0.2, 35, 16.\nGroup 10 (Lx/Dc, 200, 150): g400: 650/130/32.5/20/12.5, 7/1, 0.02, 55, 12, 5x11x5/5x11x5/5x16x5/5x11x5; g650: 1000/200/50/32/20, 8/0, 0.2, 45, 14; g1000: 1600/320/80/50/32, 8/0, 0.2, 35, 16.\nGroup 11 (all four, 200, 200): g650: 1000/200/50/32/20, 7/1, 0.02, 55, 12, 5x16x5/5x16x5/8x22x7/5x16x5; g1000: 1600/320/80/50/32, 7/1, 0.02, 45, 14; g1600: 2500/500/125/80/50, 8/0, 0.2, 35, 16.\nGroup 12 (Lx/Dc, 250, 200): same as group 9.\n\nFor brevity in matrix rows, maybe I only list values that... no — each row must be a full object. OK.\n\nHmm, given rows repeat group-level fields (dn_body, dn_cartridge, drums, control_element, bearings) with rowspan semantics, each row object should still carry all fields for completeness. Yes, flatten.\n\nNow model_level entries. Let me build the values arrays (model ids abbreviated as defined above).\n\nI'll define short type prefixes:\nA = \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc\"\nB = \"FMT-L/FMT-S/FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc\"\n\nModels in order:\nm1 A DN50/80 G100\nm2 A DN50/80 G160\nm3 A DN50/80 G250\nm4 B DN80/80 G100\nm5 B DN80/80 G160\nm6 B DN80/80 G250\nm7 A DN80/100 G160\nm8 A DN80/100 G250\nm9 A DN80/100 G400\nm10 A DN100/80 G100\nm11 A DN100/80 G160\nm12 A DN100/80 G250\nm13 B DN100/100 G160\nm14 B DN100/100 G250\nm15 B DN100/100 G400\nm16 A DN100/150 G400\nm17 A DN100/150 G650\nm18 A DN100/150 G1000\nm19 A DN150/100 G160\nm20 A DN150/100 G250\nm21 A DN150/100 G400\nm22 B DN150/150 G400\nm23 B DN150/150 G650\nm24 B DN150/150 G1000\nm25 A DN150/200 G650\nm26 A DN150/200 G1000\nm27 A DN150/200 G1600\nm28 A DN200/150 G400\nm29 A DN200/150 G650\nm30 A DN200/150 G1000\nm31 B DN200/200 G650\nm32 B DN200/200 G1000\nm33 B DN200/200 G1600\nm34 A DN250/200 G650\nm35 A DN250/200 G1000\nm36 A DN250/200 G1600\n\ng_value values: [100,160,250,100,160,250,160,250,400,100,160,250,160,250,400,400,650,1000,160,250,400,400,650,1000,650,1000,1600,400,650,1000,650,1000,1600,650,1000,1600]\n\nqmax: [160,250,400, 160,250,400, 250,400,650, 160,250,400, 250,400,650, 650,1000,1600, 250,400,650, 650,1000,1600, 1000,1600,2500, 650,1000,1600, 1000,1600,2500, 1000,1600,2500]\n\nqt: [32,50,80, 32,50,80, 50,80,130, 32,50,80, 50,80,130, 130,200,320, 50,80,130, 130,200,320, 200,320,500, 130,200,320, 200,320,500, 200,320,500]\n\nqmin_mr_1_20: [8,12.5,20, 8,12.5,20, 12.5,20,32.5, 8,12.5,20, 12.5,20,32.5, 32.5,50,80, 12.5,20,32.5, 32.5,50,80, 50,80,125, 32.5,50,80, 50,80,125, 50,80,125]\n\nqmin_mr_1_30: [5,8,12.5, 5,8,12.5, 8,12.5,20, 5,8,12.5, 8,12.5,20, 20,32,50, 8,12.5,20, 20,32,50, 32,50,80, 20,32,50, 32,50,80, 32,50,80]\n\nqmin_mr_1_50: [3,5,8, 3,5,8, 5,8,12.5, 3,5,8, 5,8,12.5, 12.5,20,32, 5,8,12.5, 12.5,20,32, 20,32,50, 12.5,20,32, 20,32,50, 20,32,50]\n\nLet me verify against the table:\nGroup 6 (m16-18): G400: 32.5/20/12.5 ✓; G650: 50/32/20 ✓; G1000: 80/50/32 ✓.\nGroup 9 (m25-27): G650: 50/32/20 ✓; G1000: 80/50/32 ✓; G1600: 125/80/50 ✓.\nGroup 10 (m28-30): G400: 32.5/20/12.5 ✓; G650: 50/32/20 ✓; G1000: 80/50/32 ✓.\nGroup 11 (m31-33): G650: 50/32/20 ✓; G1000: 80/50/32 ✓; G1600: 125/80/50 ✓.\nGroup 12 (m34-36): same as group 9 ✓.\n\nNow should I write out full model ids in each value? Yes. That's 6×36 = 216 model-id strings, each ~30 chars → ~6.5KB. OK.\n\nType-level: also worth noting the certified_type.type_designations includes all four types.\n\nAlso \"Accuracy class 1,0\" → recommendation.accuracy_classes: [1.0].\n\nShould page_total be 5? \"Page 1 of 5\" — yes, page_total: 5.\n\ndate_issued: \"2018-09-24\".\n\nThe document is a fragment of one certificate (parts 1 of 3) — fine, just extract what's present.\n\nAlso the \"scheme (A|B)\": \"B\".\n\noiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\".\n\nFootnotes: none.\n\nNow, do I include characteristics.model_level for g_value etc.? Yes as planned. Attribute naming: `g_value` (unit null), `qmax` (m³/h), `qt` (m³/h), `qmin_mr_1_20`, `qmin_mr_1_30`, `qmin_mr_1_50` (m³/h).\n\nValues must be objects with value/unit_symbol/unit_id/footnote_markers? The rule says \"Every characteristic value MUST be an object with this shape\" — the shape includes value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers. But the examples in the schema (R137 example) show values like {\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5} without unit_symbol/unit_id inside each. The units are at the attribute level (unit_symbol on the model_level entry). I'll follow the example: value objects = {model, value}. For type_level, the examples show {\"value\": ..., \"unit_symbol\": ...}. I'll include unit_symbol at the value level where relevant for type_level, and set unit_id null / omit. To be safe I'll include \"unit_id\": null? The example doesn't include it. Rule 3: \"Leave unit_id: null unless certain.\" I can include unit_id: null or omit. Examples omit it. I'll omit for cleanliness — actually rule says every value MUST be an object with that shape... I'll include unit_symbol where applicable and footnote_markers when present, omitting unit_id (it says \"Leave unit_id: null\" — implying include it as null). Hmm. To comply strictly, include \"unit_id\": null in type_level entries. That's fine.\n\nFor model_level values, the example shows {\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5} — no unit objects. I'll follow the example exactly (units at attribute level).\n\nNow, model_family.models: 36 entries {\"model_id\": \"...\"}. \n\nLet me also consider whether to put \"label\". I'll add label like \"FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc, DN 50 body / 80 cartridge, G100\"? That's a lot of duplication. Skip label. Just model_id.\n\nmatrix_tables[0]:\nname: \"General characteristics of the family of instruments (Table 2)\"\ndescription: \"Flattened from the source table with rowspan/colspan resolved. Each row is one meter variant: Type × DN body × DN cartridge × G-value. Qmin is listed per measurement-ratio (MR) option: MR 1:20 applies over 0–101 bar(g), MR 1:30 over 4–101 bar(g), MR 1:50 over 8–101 bar(g). Bearing dimensions are inside diameter × outside diameter × width.\"\nfootnotes: []\n\nWait — the MR pressure ranges: \"MR 1:20 0..101 bar(g)\", \"MR 1:30 4...101 bar(g)\", \"MR 1:50 8..101 bar(g)\". Yes.\n\nAlso Table 1 as matrix_table? No — Table 1 is flat key-value, captured in type_level.\n\nNow assemble final JSON. Also note applicants[] entry: name \"Flow Meter Group B.V.\" manufacturers: same entity.\n\ncertified_type.description: \"Turbine gas meter\" — from \"A turbine gas meter\".\n\nLet me also include characteristics.type_level entries with unit_id: null. Values as strings for class/orientation (allowed since they're non-numeric).\n\nWrite the JSON now. Be careful with numbers: 12.5, 32.5, 0.02, 0.2, 2.5 etc.\n\nModel ids: use exact strings. I'll use \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN50/80 G100\" style.\n\nActually, hmm, maybe better model_id format: \"DN50/80 G100 (FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc)\". Either fine. I'll keep \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN50/80 G100\".\n\nLet me now write out everything.\n\nFor type_level, keys:\n- destined_for_measurement_of: {value: \"Gas volume\", unit_symbol: null, unit_id: null}\n- mechanical_class: {value: \"M1\", unit_symbol: null, unit_id: null}\n- electromagnetic_class: {value: \"Not applicable (the meter has no electronics)\", unit_symbol: null, unit_id: null}\n- ambient_temperature_range: {value: {min: -40, max: 70}, unit_symbol: \"°C\", unit_id: null}\n- gas_temperature_range: {value: {min: -40, max: 70}, unit_symbol: \"°C\", unit_id: null}\n- orientation: {value: \"Horizontal / Vertical up / Vertical down (all orientations)\", unit_symbol: null, unit_id: null}\n- flow_direction: {value: \"Uni-directional (indicated with arrow)\", unit_symbol: null, unit_id: null}\n- pressure_range? Hmm — the MR pressure ranges 0..101 bar(g) relate to Qmin conditions, not overall meter pressure. The meter MPM? Not stated as overall characteristic. Skip at type level; captured in matrix description.\n\nmodel_level entries (6): each with attribute, unit_symbol, unit_id? The example shows just attribute, unit_symbol, values. I'll include unit_symbol and omit unit_id at this level (example omitted). Actually to be consistent-ish I'll include \"unit_symbol\" only, matching example.\n\nNow the big JSON. Let me write it carefully.\n\nAlso include test_reports role \"type_evaluation_report\" for the five reports and \"documentation_file\" for T10466-7.\n\nCertificate:\n```json\n\"certificate\": {\n  \"number\": \"R137/2012-B-NL1-18.05\",\n  \"scheme\": \"B\",\n  \"project_number\": \"1901860\",\n  \"page_total\": 5,\n  \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\",\n  \"date_issued\": \"2018-09-24\",\n  \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n}\n```\n\nNow write matrix rows fully. Let me carefully produce each row object.\n\nCommon bearings sets:\nS1 = std front \"3x6x3\", std rear \"2x5x2\", opt front \"3x8x4\", opt rear \"3x6x3\" (groups 1,2,4)\nS2 = \"3x8x4\", \"3x6x3\", \"5x10x3\", \"3x6x3\" (groups 3,5,7)\nS3 = \"5x11x5\", \"5x11x5\", \"5x16x5\", \"5x11x5\" (groups 6,8,10)\nS4 = \"5x16x5\", \"5x16x5\", \"8x22x7\", \"5x16x5\" (groups 9,11,12)\n\nOK. Writing final JSON now. It'll be long; that's expected.\n\nOne more check: number_of_drums \"7 / 1\" split into before/after comma: 7 and 1; \"8 / 0\": 8 and 0.\n\nControl element: 0.02 (for 7/1) and 0.2 (for 8/0).\n\nBlade angle degree: 55/45/35 per row; blade qty 12/14/16.\n\nDouble-check group 6 row 2: \"650 | 1000 | 200 | 50 | 32 | 20 — 7 / 1 | 0,02 — 45 | 14\" ✓ (from markdown: `<tr><td>650</td><td>1000</td><td>200</td><td>50</td><td>32</td><td>20</td><td>7 / 1</td><td>0,02</td><td>45</td><td>14</td></tr>`).\n\nGroup 10 row 2: `<td>650</td><td>1000</td><td>200</td><td>50</td><td>32</td><td>20</td><td>8 / 0</td><td>0,2</td><td>45</td><td>14</td>` ✓ 8/0, 0.2.\n\nGroup 11 row 2: `<td>1000</td><td>1600</td><td>320</td><td>80</td><td>50</td><td>32</td><td>7 / 1</td><td>0,02</td><td>45</td><td>14</td>` ✓ 7/1, 0.02.\n\nGroup 12 row 2: `8 / 0, 0,2, 45, 14` ✓.\n\nGroup 9 row 2: `<td>1000</td><td>1600</td><td>320</td><td>80</td><td>50</td><td>32</td><td>8 / 0</td><td>0,2</td><td>45</td><td>14</td>` ✓.\n\nGood.\n\nNow, output. No markdown fences, just JSON.\n\nLet me now write the whole thing. I need to be meticulous.\n\nFor the models array, model ids:\n1 \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN50/80 G100\"\n2 \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN50/80 G160\"\n3 \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN50/80 G250\"\n4 \"FMT-L/FMT-S/FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN80/80 G100\"\n5 \"FMT-L/FMT-S/FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN80/80 G160\"\n6 \"FMT-L/FMT-S/FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN80/80 G250\"\n7 \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN80/100 G160\"\n8 \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN80/100 G250\"\n9 \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN80/100 G400\"\n10 \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN100/80 G100\"\n11 \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN100/80 G160\"\n12 \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN100/80 G250\"\n13 \"FMT-L/FMT-S/FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN100/100 G160\"\n14 \"FMT-L/FMT-S/FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN100/100 G250\"\n15 \"FMT-L/FMT-S/FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN100/100 G400\"\n16 \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN100/150 G400\"\n17 \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN100/150 G650\"\n18 \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN100/150 G1000\"\n19 \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN150/100 G160\"\n20 \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN150/100 G250\"\n21 \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN150/100 G400\"\n22 \"FMT-L/FMT-S/FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN150/150 G400\"\n23 \"FMT-L/FMT-S/FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN150/150 G650\"\n24 \"FMT-L/FMT-S/FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN150/150 G1000\"\n25 \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN150/200 G650\"\n26 \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN150/200 G1000\"\n27 \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN150/200 G1600\"\n28 \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN200/150 G400\"\n29 \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN200/150 G650\"\n30 \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN200/150 G1000\"\n31 \"FMT-L/FMT-S/FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN200/200 G650\"\n32 \"FMT-L/FMT-S/FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN200/200 G1000\"\n33 \"FMT-L/FMT-S/FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN200/200 G1600\"\n34 \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN250/200 G650\"\n35 \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN250/200 G1000\"\n36 \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN250/200 G1600\"\n\nOK, writing final answer JSON now.",
      "signature": "427d97097c4548839a101b10"
